# Vendor property types.
#
# Why this file exists: a diff of the built vendor_property_contexts against the
# stock one showed 34 prefixes that stock labels specifically and this tree did
# not, so every one of them fell through to vendor_default_prop.
#
# That is not merely untidy. system/sepolicy/private/property.te carries
#
#     neverallow { domain -init -vendor_init } vendor_default_prop:property_service set;
#
# so any domain other than init/vendor_init that needs to *write* one of those
# properties is permanently denied, and no rule anywhere can grant it. Three of
# the seven groups below are exactly that case:
#
#   vendor_cam_prop     hal_camera_default writes the camera OTP serial numbers
#   vendor_dolbyv_prop  dvs-aidl-service writes its own state
#   vendor_hw_ai_prop   hal_lenovo_ai_proc writes its own state
#
# The other four are read-mostly and matter less, but they cost one line each
# and collapsing unrelated subsystems into a single bucket is a real, if soft,
# privilege-separation regression: "read the camera serial number" would also
# imply "read the AI HAL's and Dolby's state".
#
# Types and prefixes are taken verbatim from the stock vendor_property_contexts
# and vendor_sepolicy.cil.
#
# Deliberately NOT carried over: vendor_pcsys_prop (3 prefixes,
# vendor.zuxos.pcengine.*). PC Engine is dropped from this port — its container
# launcher /vendor/bin/lrootfs.sh and lrootfs.rc are no longer extracted, and the
# app that drove it lived on ZUI's product partition, which PixelOS replaces.
# Labelling properties for a subsystem that cannot run would be noise.

vendor_internal_prop(vendor_lenovotb_init_prop)
vendor_internal_prop(vendor_cam_prop)
vendor_internal_prop(vendor_dolbyv_prop)
vendor_internal_prop(vendor_hw_ai_prop)
vendor_internal_prop(vendor_check_keymaster_prop)
vendor_internal_prop(vendor_arcsoft_faceunlock)
vendor_internal_prop(vendor_thermal_prop)
